<tr>
    <td  bgcolor="#c0c0c0" valign="TOP"><table width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="5" border="0">
<tr>
    <td  bgcolor="#c0c0c0" width="100%" valign="top"><tt><font color="#000000">
Author:&nbsp;<a href="/showmember.php?name=Jiggles">Jiggles</a>&nbsp;(---.dsl.rcsntx.swbell.net)<br>
Date:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;06-17-01 11:32<br>
<br><HTML>I am taking the thread from the earlier post and cleaning it up here:<br>
<br>
<br>
<b>Membership</b><br>
I'm going to chirp in on this again.  When I first joined this guild, and became an officer down the road, I got so hyped up about X issues, etc etc.  In the amount of time from then to know I've come to realize that almost any rule you come up with for how you want a group of people to act within a game is almost impossible.  People play games to have fun, and games like MMORPG's are even more loose when it comes to how people play it.<br>
<br>
When you think of how to structure the guild, or the rules by which we play.. you must think of how it can be enforced.  If you come up wit a great idea, but it can't be enforced in any shape or form, it will eventually die out or become completely forgotten = Wasted time, and added frustration.<br>
<br>
Take the rule: "If an officer invites a member into the guild, they must put them onto the website."  There are issues that arise with that rule, or with the website altogether that lead to our membership problems.  It was a rule tossed together to keep our membership information in line.<br>
<ol><li>An officer must remember to actually add the person.<b>Somewhat enforceable</b></li><br>
<li>That new member must log in and update their character on a regular basis, notify us if they are in or out of the guild, etc.  ie. PARTICIPATE.  <b>As you can't force people to play, it is not enforceable at all</b></li><br>
<li>The officers must keep track of all characters and update the website when they come into new information.  <b>Randomly enforceable.  Not all characters log in at same time, not all people have access to website while playing, etc</b></li><br>
</ol><br>
<br>
In the end, the database end sup with characters never updated, logged into, or even added.  Either way, I had to clean up the database best I could.  From the statistics of how many characters I have deleted out of our database (@40), the average level on those characters was 5.  This tells me that we either have<br>
<ol><li>Newbies who were just trying out their characters for a bit and have ot gone away without letting us know.</li><br>
<li>Characters who are still played, not in our guild anymore.</li><br>
<li>Some characters who are in the guild, still played (rarely), and never logged into the website to update their character and hence idle</li><br>
</ol><br>
<br>
<b>Honor</b><br>
<b>1</b> If we are to judge who is honorable, and who isn't.. that requires that we see active participation by our members, and our members (who are honorable themselves) give feedback from playing with these people on a regular basis that they are honorable.  The average level of all our active players now is beginning to bottom out around 20.  So for us to be actively watching those who we think are good candidates, we'd have to be grouping up with them.  So that means a good portion of our candidates should be around lvl 20.<br>
<br>
<b>2</b> In addition, it is true that if you go on a recruiting fair in Greater Faydark, it is almost certain you are going to get at least one recruit.  75% of the time, that newbie recruit ends up quitting the guild once they understand what one is, or they quit their character once they know they don't like it or the game.  <br>
<br>
<b>3</b> To keep the good name of being honorable, how can we effectively determine someone to be a player in good standing and honorable at lvl 1?  10+ sure, because you have now had time with that person (hopefully) to determine that, even though it is quite possible to power level a character from 1 to 10 in a matter of 3-4 hours.<br>
<br>
<p><br>
In conclusion, if we are to set standards or rules for how our guild is to act, therefore proposing a future plan for how our guild is supposed to look like, we must make them reasonable, enforceable, and practical.  If we want to make sure we keep a group of honorable players, we must also make sure to lessen our attrition as much as possible so that we can keep the honorable people.. and not just invite people because of a quota.<br>
<br>
-Jiggs</HTML></font></tt><p>
